      "message": "Gemini generated code. Check for correctness:\n\n----\n\nTo implement compile-time span extents, we need to update the function signatures in the cryptography utility files to strictly require the correct byte lengths. This allows us to delete the runtime size checks entirely.\n\nBecause the caller (`bootrom.cc`) parses dynamically sized network buffers, we also need to slightly update how it extracts the IV and Tag so that it generates fixed-extent spans that the compiler can verify.\n\nHere are the exact changes needed across the three files:\n\n### 1. `util/egis/crypto_util.h`\n\nUpdate the function signatures to enforce the constant sizes `kAes256KeySize`, `kAesGcmIvSize`, and `kAesGcmTagSize` defined at the top of the file.\n\n**Current:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e GenerateGcmIv(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e iv_buf);\n\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e AesGcmEncrypt(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e key, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e iv,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e aad, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e plaintext,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e ciphertext, std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e tag);\n\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e AesGcmDecrypt(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e key, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e iv,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e aad, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e ciphertext,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e tag, std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e plaintext);\n\n```\n\n**New:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e GenerateGcmIv(\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t, kAesGcmIvSize\u003e iv_buf);\n\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e AesGcmEncrypt(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, kAes256KeySize\u003e key,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, kAesGcmIvSize\u003e iv,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e aad, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e plaintext,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e ciphertext,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t, kAesGcmTagSize\u003e tag);\n\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e AesGcmDecrypt(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, kAes256KeySize\u003e key,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, kAesGcmIvSize\u003e iv,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e aad, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e ciphertext,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, kAesGcmTagSize\u003e tag,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e plaintext);\n\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. `util/egis/crypto_util.cc`\n\nUpdate the signatures to match the header, and entirely remove the manual `if (key.size() !\u003d ...)` checks at the top of the encrypt and decrypt functions. The compiler now handles this constraint natively.\n\n**Current (Excerpt):**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e AesGcmEncrypt(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e key, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e iv,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e aad, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e plaintext,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e ciphertext, std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e tag) {\n  if (key.size() !\u003d kAes256KeySize || iv.size() !\u003d kAesGcmIvSize ||\n      tag.size() !\u003d kAesGcmTagSize) {\n    return std::unexpected(CryptoError::kInvalidParam);\n  }\n// ...\n\n```\n\n**New (Excerpt):**\n\n```cpp\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e GenerateGcmIv(\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t, kAesGcmIvSize\u003e iv_buf) {\n  if (RAND_bytes(iv_buf.data(), iv_buf.size()) !\u003d 1) {\n    return std::unexpected(CryptoError::kRngFailed);\n  }\n  return {};\n}\n\nstd::expected\u003cvoid, CryptoError\u003e AesGcmEncrypt(\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, kAes256KeySize\u003e key,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, kAesGcmIvSize\u003e iv,\n    std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e aad, std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e plaintext,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e ciphertext,\n    std::span\u003cuint8_t, kAesGcmTagSize\u003e tag) {\n  \n  EvpCipherCtxPtr ctx(EVP_CIPHER_CTX_new(), \u0026::EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free);\n  if (!ctx) return std::unexpected(CryptoError::kContextAllocationFailed);\n  // ... rest of function remains identical\n\n```\n\n*(Apply the exact same signature change and removal of the `if` check to `AesGcmDecrypt`)*.\n\n---\n\n### 3. `util/egis/bootrom.cc`\n\nIn `SendBootromCommandAes`, you extract the IV, Tag, and Ciphertext from the USB response buffer. The standard `subspan(offset, count)` method returns a *dynamic* span. To pass these to our newly strict decryption function, we must use the templated versions `.first\u003cN\u003e()` and `.subspan\u003cOffset, N\u003e()`, which return compile-time fixed spans.\n\n**Current (Excerpt in `SendBootromCommandAes`):**\n\n```cpp\n  size_t offset \u003d 0;\n  auto rsp_iv \u003d secure_data-\u003esubspan(offset, crypto::kAesGcmIvSize);\n  offset +\u003d crypto::kAesGcmIvSize;\n\n  auto rsp_tag \u003d secure_data-\u003esubspan(offset, crypto::kAesGcmTagSize);\n  offset +\u003d crypto::kAesGcmTagSize;\n\n  auto rsp_ciphertext \u003d secure_data-\u003esubspan(offset);\n\n```\n\n**New (Excerpt):**\n\n```cpp\n  // Use templated span extractors to enforce sizes at compile-time\n  auto rsp_iv \u003d secure_data-\u003efirst\u003ccrypto::kAesGcmIvSize\u003e();\n  \n  auto rsp_tag \u003d secure_data-\u003esubspan\u003ccrypto::kAesGcmIvSize, crypto::kAesGcmTagSize\u003e();\n  \n  auto rsp_ciphertext \u003d secure_data-\u003esubspan(crypto::kAesGcmIvSize + crypto::kAesGcmTagSize);\n\n```\n\nBecause `aes_key_` is already defined as a `std::array\u003cuint8_t, 32\u003e` in your class, it will automatically and safely convert to the required `std::span\u003cconst uint8_t, 32\u003e` when passed to the encrypt/decrypt functions. No changes are needed there.",
